1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wang Chang 王昶 (d. 259), ''zi'' Wenshu 文舒, Three States period Wei writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Chang’s ancestral home was Jinyang 晉陽 in Taiyuan 太原 (modern Taiyuan, Shanxi). During his youth he and Wang Ling 王淩 (172–251) were equally famous in Jinyang. When Cao Pi 曹丕 (187–226) was heir designate, Wang Chang served on hi
2 CONC s staff as an instructor and palace cadet. When Cao Pi assumed the Wei throne in 220, he appointed Wang Chang gentleman cavalier attendant. He then was appointed agriculture director for the capital of Luoyang, in which capacity h
2 CONC e opening up additional land for cultivation. His next position was regional inspector of Yanzhou 兗州 (administrative seat Linqiu 廩丘, northeast of modern Echeng 鄂城, Shandong), where he met Ruan Ji 阮籍 (210–263). However, Ruan Ji ref
2 CONC used to speak to Wang Chang for an entire day.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 230, Emperor Ming issued an edict ordering the recruitment of men of learning and talent. Sima Yi 司馬懿 (179–251) recommended Wang Chang. In the Zhengshi period (240–249) he was promoted to General Who Chastizes the South, and wa
2 CONC s given command over military affairs in Jingzhou 荊州 (administrative seat modern Xinye 新野, Henan) and Yuzhou 豫州 (modern Anhui). In 250, Sima Yi seized de facto control over the court, and Wang Chang presented various proposals con
2 CONC cerning examinations and promoting frugality at the court. As a reward for a successful military expedition against Wu, Wang Chang was rewarded with the title of General-in-chief Who Chastizes the South and was granted the noble t
2 CONC itle of Marquis of Jingling. When in 255 Guanqiu Jian 毌丘儉 (d. 255) and Zhuge Dan 諸葛誕 (d. 258) staged a coup against Sima Yi, Wang Chang successfully led a force against them. He was then promoted to cavalry general. In 258, he was
2 CONC  appointed minister of works. Wang Chang died in 259 at the age over seventy. He was buried east of Gongling 恭陵 in the Beimang Mountains north of Luoyang.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Chang’s son Wang Hun 王渾 (223–297) was a distinguished statesman. His grandson Wang Ji 王濟 (ca. 247–ca. 292) was a prominent writer and official during the Western Jin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The monographs on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' and the two Tang histories list Wang Chang’s collected works in five ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Yan Kejun collects eight of his prose writings in ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qi
2 CONC an Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''. While serving as regional inspector in Yanzhou, Wang Chang composed over twenty essays on political thought titled ''Zhi lun'' 治論 (Disquisitions on government). He also wrote over ten essays on the mil
2 CONC itary strategy of ''qi'' 奇 (surprise) and ''zheng'' 正 (directness). He called these works ''Bing shu'' (Writings on warfare). He presented them to the imperial court in the Qinglong period (233–237). They have not survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Chang’s biography in the ''Sanguo zhi'' contains a long letter of moral instructions for his sons. He urged them to follow the model of Xu Gan 徐幹 (171–217/218) and Ren Gu 任嘏 (fl. 200).
2 CONT
2 CONT David R. Knechtges
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
2 CONT * 王昶 Wang Chang, Brill’s Chinese Reference Shelf. Brill Online, 2017. http://chinesereferenceshelf.brillonline.com/ancient-literature/entries/COM-0146
