0 @N6000000002188099910@ NOTE Wu Zetian
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Wu Zetian 
1 CONT Birth and death: 625¹–December 16, 705 
1 CONT Family name: Wu (?) 
1 CONT Given name: Mei² (?), later Zhao³ (?) 
1 CONT Dates of reign: Oct. 19, 6904–Feb. 22, 7055 
1 CONT Temple name: None6 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (short) Empress Zetian7 (????) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (full) Empress Zetian Shunsheng8
1 CONT ?????? 
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar.
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar.
1 CONT ———
1 CONT 1. Year deduced from the age at death given in the
1 CONT New Book of Tang (???), compiled in 1045-1060, which
1 CONT is the year favored by modern historians. The year of birth
1 CONT deduced from the Book of Tang (???), compiled in
1 CONT 941-945, is 623. The year of birth deducted from the
1 CONT Comprehensive Mirror compiled in 1065-84, is 624.
1 CONT 2. Was given this name by Emperor Taizong in the late 630s
1 CONT after she had entered the imperial palace (see inside article).
1 CONT 3. Had this Chinese character created in December 689 and
1 CONT chose it as her given name. Became her taboo name when she
1 CONT ascended the throne the next year. Some sources assert that
1 CONT this character was actually written ? . Some sources also
1 CONT assert that her original given name was Zhao (?) , and that
1 CONT in 689 she only changed the character to write her name, but
1 CONT this is confirmed neither by the Book of Tang, nor by the
1 CONT New Book of Tang, which both do not record her original
1 CONT given name, if she had any.
1 CONT 4. Was already partially in control of power since around
1 CONT 660, and totally since January 665 (see inside article). Zhou
1 CONT dynasty was proclaimed on October 16, 690, and she
1 CONT proclaimed herself emperor on October 19, demoting her
1 CONT younger son Emperor Ruizong to the rank of imperial heir.
1 CONT 5. Lost power at the palace coup of February 20, 705. Then
1 CONT on February 22 was forced to transfer the imperial dignity
1 CONT onto her older son, restored as Emperor Zhongzong
1 CONT on February 23
1 CONT 6. Zhou dynasty was abolished before her death, and she
1 CONT was reverted to the rank of empress consort on her death, so
1 CONT that she does not have a temple name. In China, empresses
1 CONT consort, unlike ruling emperors, are not given a temple name.
1 CONT 7. Zetian was the beginning of the honorific name (??) 
1 CONT given to her in February 705 by her son the restored emperor
1 CONT Zhongzong. The honorific name was used as her posthumous
1 CONT name when she died ten months later.
1 CONT 8. Final version of her posthumous name as given in July 749. 
1 CONT Wu Zetian (??? pinyin : Wu Zétian) (625 - December 16, 705), personal name Wu Zhao (??), was the only woman in the history of China to assume the title of Emperor (Huangdi; ??). Ruling China first through puppet emperors from 665 
1 CONC to 690, which wasn't unprecedented in Chinese history, she then broke all precedents when she founded her own dynasty in 690, the Zhou (?), and ruled personally under the name Emperor Shengshen (????) from 690 to 705. Her rise and
1 CONC  reign has been criticized harshly by Confucian historians but has been viewed under a different light after the 1950s.
