1 CONT Zhongzong (November 26, 656 – July 3, 710), personal name Li Xian, was the fourth Emperor of the Tang Dynasty of China, ruling briefly in 684 and again from 705 to 710.
1 CONT
1 CONT Zhongzong was the son of Emperor Gaozong of Tang and Empress Wu. He succeeded his father in 684. His mother, however, deposed him less than two months later in favor of his younger brother Emperor Ruizong. Zhongzong, demoted to a 
1 CONC princely rank, was sent in exile in the provinces and placed under house arrest. Six years later, Ruizong in turn relinquished the throne to his mother and Wu officially proclaimed herself "Emperor", while Ruizong was made crown p
1 CONC rince.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 698 the court was caught in the middle of a bitter power struggle. The Empress Wu and her nephew, Wu Sansi (???), ran the court with a great deal of corruption. In an attempt to assuage oponents at court, Empress Wu liberated Z
1 CONC hongzong from his fourteen years of seclusion and recalled him to the capiptal in April 698. Zhongzong was reinstated as crown prince in October 698, taking the place of his brother Ruizong. On February 20, 705 a palace coup depos
1 CONC ed Empress Wu and Zhonzong was restored as Emperor three days later. Emperor Zhongzong reigned for five years but was a rather weak and easily influenced ruler. Real power was in the hands of his empress consort, Empress Wei (???)
1 CONC , and Empress Wu's nephew, Wu Sansi, who was the lover of Empress Wei.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 710, Emperor Zhongzong died, allegedly poisoned by Empress Wei who then installed Zhongzong's only surviving son, Li Chongmao, as emperor. Empress Wei, who had failed to install her daughter with Zhongzong, the Princess Anle (?
1 CONC ???), as heir to Zhongzong, thought that Li Chongmao (known posthumously as Emperor Shang), born of Zhongzong and a low ranking concubine, and who was only 16 years old, would be easy to control and allow her to preserve her power
1 CONC . The scheme failed, however, when Princess Taiping, the sister of Zhongzong, launched a coup two weeks later with her nephew Li Longji (later Emperor Xuanzong), son of the abdicated Emperor Ruizong, and overthrew Empress Wei and 
1 CONC the young emperor (Princess Taiping said to the young emperor:" you shall not sit on the emperor's seat"). Ruizong, the father of Li Longji and the older brother of Princess Taiping, was restored as emperor.
1 CONT
